{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime bias: when the last 5–10 days show a sustained upswing (higher highs / rising 10d MA), the model is systematically too low across the first ~15–20 forecast days. Apply an upward level shift of roughly +1 to +3 €/t (stronger in the first half of the horizon).",
    "Turning-point lag: the model reacts ~2–5 trading days late to sharp accelerations (breakouts). If the last 3–5 days’ return is strongly positive, steepen the near-term path (days 1–10) rather than keeping it flat.",
    "Post-peak crash underestimation: after a multi-week rally followed by a clear reversal (large negative daily moves / break below short MA), the model remains too high in the back half of the horizon (days ~15–30). Add stronger mean-reversion downward adjustment (often −2 to −6 €/t by day 30).",
    "Overshoot on renewed strength: in late examples the model projects continued strength into days 20–30 (sometimes >73–74) while realized prices mean-revert lower. Cap long-horizon upside by anchoring days 20–30 closer to the last observed price or 20d average (reduce slope).",
    "Horizon asymmetry: apply corrections that are front-loaded in rising regimes (boost days 1–15) and back-loaded in falling regimes (cut days 15–30). Avoid uniform shifts across all 30 days.",
    "Volatility compression: the model’s forecast range is too narrow during rallies (misses higher highs) but too wide on the upside late in the horizon after reversals. Increase near-term volatility allowance in breakouts; dampen long-horizon volatility via stronger pull-to-mean."
  ]
}
